        "teacherMoves": "1. Write on the board: 'A RATIONAL NUMBER is any number that can be written as p/q, where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0.' Underline 'integers' and 'q ≠ 0'. Say: 'Every number in Sister Wanjiku's left column is rational. Let us prove it together.'\n2. Walk through 37.1 = 371/10 and 120 = 120/1 as worked examples, narrating each step.\n3. Say: 'Now here is a new list — eight numbers from everyday Kenyan life. For each one, write: RATIONAL or IRRATIONAL, and one sentence explaining why.' Display the eight numbers. Allow 5 minutes of individual work.\n4. WAIT TIME: After the 5 minutes, pause for 12 seconds before initiating cold-calls — this gives learners time to review their answers.\n5. Cold-call 4 learners (different learners from the Observe phase): (i) 'Njeri, is 2/7 rational? Tell us why.' (ii) 'Otieno, what about √9 — rational or irrational? Surprise us.' (iii) 'Fatuma, the price KSh 55.50 — rational or irrational?' (iv) 'Baraka, what about π ≈ 3.142 written on a road-marking stencil — and does the approximation change anything?'\n6. After each response, ask the class: 'Do you agree or disagree? Thumbs up or thumbs sideways.' Address any disagreement immediately.\n7. Return to the phenomenon: 'So which column does √2 — the tile diagonal — belong in? Why can we NOT write it as p/q? We will prove this in Lesson 3, but for now, mark it IRRATIONAL and put a question mark next to it on your DQB.'",
